From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] Applications in /data are compiled from their own profile after a system update

Patch 0026 has the applications whose code is in /data compiled again when a new
system brings a new boot image, with the filter they were installed with
(pm.dexopt.install, "speed": every method). That is the right filter for an
application that has never run, and the wrong one for an application the device
has been reading with for a week: by then ART has written down which methods and
classes that actually takes.

Measured on Test V52 with WeRead and the profile of eight days' reading, three
paired cold starts each way with the page cache dropped:

                      compiling    compiled code    cold start (median)
  speed                  1410 s           180 MB                17017 ms
  speed-profile           181 s            75 MB                15754 ms

Compiling from the profile is better on all three counts, and the first is the
one the installer has to warn about today ("the first start takes about 23
minutes"). The start is faster with a twentieth of the code compiled because a
cold start here reads 255-271 MB and the storage, not the CPU, is what it waits
for; the profile also lets dex2oat write an application image of the classes the
application loads at startup (dalvik.vm.appimageformat), which "speed" cannot.

So the filter for these applications becomes pm.dexopt.ab-ota, which AOSP uses
when a new system means everything has to be compiled again and which pw3.mk
sets, as AOSP does, to "speed-profile". This device has no A/B update; the
reason is named for how AOSP delivers one, and describes exactly this moment.
pm.dexopt.install stays "speed" for the install itself, where there is no
profile yet, and pm.dexopt.bg-dexopt stays "speed" for the reason below.

Two details decide whether this holds up:

  - An application with no profile would be compiled to nothing at all, which on
    this CPU means unusable. The reference profile is readable only by the
    application's own user, so the decision is made in installd, which opens it:
    frameworks/native patch 0006, asked for here with
    DEXOPT_SPEED_IF_NO_PROFILE. Falling back costs nothing that is not lost
    today.
  - What the application has recorded since the last compilation is in its
    current profile, and dex2oat reads the reference profile. The two are merged
    by the job that runs when idle and charging, and deliberately not here. AOSP
    leaves the merge out of boot to save the time it takes (b/28612421); the
    stronger reason is that a merge which finds new use makes getDexOptNeeded
    ask for a recompilation, so a boot on which nothing else had to be compiled
    -- every update whose boot image happens to be unchanged -- would spend
    three minutes compiling. What is read here is the reference profile as that
    job last left it. Where there is none, installd compiles everything, which
    is what happens today.

An application updated by the user has its profiles cleared by
clearAppProfilesLIF, so a profile never outlives the code it describes.

The job that compiles when the device is idle on the charger keeps
pm.dexopt.bg-dexopt, which pw3.mk sets to "speed-profile", because that job is
the only thing that ever writes a reference profile: isProfileUpdated() returns
without merging unless the filter it is given compiles from one. It is not
given the fallback this patch asks for, though. That fallback would reach the
applications that came with the system, built with "quicken" and without a
profile until they are opened, and compile every one of them; and theirs is the
one case where a profile can outlive the code it describes, since an update
replaces /system while /data keeps the profile and nothing clears it
(clearAppProfilesLIF only runs when a package is replaced), so it could not be
relied on for them anyway. The applications in /data, the ones the fallback is
for, keep their profiles across a system update because their code does not
change with it.

